<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Genius Innovations – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Genius Innovations</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Simplify explanations of common phenomena. Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in group projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify explanations of business concepts for classmates using hand gestures or examples (e.g., "Marketing is like making a movie trailer; it's designed to grab people's attention while showing a sample video ad.") Can resolve misunderstandings in group discussions on business plans by rephrasing ideas and using simple visual examples (e.g., points to survey questions as an example, and says, "Market research means finding out what people want to buy.") Can clarify tasks in group projects on entrepreneurship by using a checklist to make sure everyone understands their part (e.g., "We need to describe our product and explain why people will want it.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various business terms (e.g., 'marketing,' 'customer base,' 'automation,' 'visibility') on flashcards or the board. Students work in pairs to quickly explain one term to their partner as simply as possible, as if their partner knows nothing about business. Teacher encourages the use of examples or gestures. The teacher asks, "What strategy did you use to make your partner understand?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simplifying a complex business concept, such as "economies of scale," to the class. For example: "Economies of scale? It's like buying in bulk. If you make a lot of products, the 'cost' of each individual product goes 'down.' Think about a 'factory launch' – the more they 'produce,' the cheaper each item becomes. Notice the long 'o' sound in 'cost' and 'launch' /ɔː/." The teacher uses hand gestures and visual aids (e.g., drawing on the board). The teacher then models how to rephrase a sentence for clarity: "Original: 'The synergistic integration of resources will foster market penetration.' Simplified: 'If we combine our efforts effectively, we can reach more customers.'" The teacher also demonstrates using professional language and showing respect for suggestions: "Your idea adds an interesting approach."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to simplify explanations when talking about business ideas?" (To ensure everyone understands and can collaborate effectively).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When would you need to rephrase an idea during a group project?" (When someone looks confused, or when a task isn't clear).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (or transcript) between two students working on a business plan. One student is struggling to understand a concept (e.g., 'market research' or 'customer engagement'), and the other student simplifies the explanation, rephrases ideas, and clarifies tasks using examples and target grammar (first conditional, present perfect, future continuous).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The ability to simplify and rephrase complex information from a business context for a peer audience to ensure mutual understanding and task clarity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue and match key business terms (e.g., 'marketing strategy,' 'target customer') with their simplified definitions provided in a short multiple-choice format.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify three specific phrases or strategies the helpful student uses to clarify information or resolve misunderstandings. (e.g., "Market research means finding out what people want to buy.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students answer true/false questions about how certain tasks were clarified in the dialogue (e.g., "The student used a checklist to clarify tasks.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying explanations, resolving misunderstandings, clarifying tasks. Use of First conditional ("If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers."), Present perfect for innovations ("Local shops have begun using social media to tell more people about their products."), Future continuous ("I'll be analyzing the market trends."). Vocabulary: innovation, business, strategy, marketing, customer base, automation, efficiency, costs, promotional events, visibility, success, collaboration, entrepreneur. Pronunciation: Practice /ɔː/ (cost, talk, launch) and reduced vowels (renewable, successful). Pragmatic: Asking about business strategies, sharing ideas. Sociolinguistic: Professional language, showing respect for suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice): Simplify the Statement.</b> Students receive a worksheet with 5-6 complex sentences related to business plans (e.g., "To augment our market share, we must implement a multifaceted 'promotional events' strategy."). In pairs, they rewrite each sentence into a simpler, clearer version, ensuring target vocabulary (e.g., 'promotional events,' 'strategy,' 'market share') is used appropriately, and focusing on the /ɔː/ sound in words like 'launch.' For example: "If we host many 'promotional events,' we can 'increase' our 'visibility' and reach more customers."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice): Clarification Role-Play.</b> In small groups of three, students are given cards with either a 'complex business concept' (e.g., 'customer lifetime value,' 'supply chain management') or a 'task statement' (e.g., 'Develop a robust 'marketing strategy' for the product line'). One student reads the card, another student pretends to be confused, and the third student mediates by simplifying the concept or clarifying the task, using first conditionals to explain potential outcomes ("If we understand customer lifetime value, we 'will be developing' more 'customer-focused' 'strategies'"), present perfect for existing innovations, and future continuous for plans. They practice expressing respect for suggestions: "Your idea for a 'marketing strategy' adds an interesting approach."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction/Peer Check): Business Plan Rephrasing.</b> Students bring their draft business plan ideas from previous lessons. In pairs, one student explains a section of their plan (e.g., product description, 'marketing strategy,' 'revenue model') while the partner listens for any unclear points. The partner asks clarifying questions using pragmatic language (e.g., "What strategies have increased 'customer engagement'?"), and the first student rephrases and simplifies. The second student provides feedback on clarity and the use of professional language and checks for correct pronunciation of multisyllabic words like 'successful' /səkˈses.fəl/ and 'renewable' /rɪˈnuː.ə.bəl/.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Business Plan Explanation & Clarification Session."</b> Students work in their established business plan groups. Each group presents a key section of their "My Genius Innovations" business plan (e.g., product/service, 'marketing strategy,' 'customer base' analysis, 'innovation') to another group. The goal is not just to present, but to actively ensure the receiving group *fully understands* every concept and task. They must be prepared to simplify complex explanations, rephrase ideas, clarify tasks using examples or visual aids (e.g., pointing to survey questions, showing an ad design), and resolve any misunderstandings. They should use first conditionals to explain potential 'success' ("If we 'launch' this 'app,' we 'will be increasing' our 'engagement'"), present perfect for 'advances' already made ("We 'have developed' a unique 'marketing strategy'"), and future continuous for planned actions ("We 'will be analyzing' 'market trends' next month"). Professional language for business ideas should be evident ("This 'strategy' could 'attract' more customers to our project.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group-to-group interactive presentation and Q&A. Each group will have designated presenters and a 'mediator' who focuses on ensuring clarity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in mediating business concepts, collaborative interaction, effective task completion through clarification, and appropriate use of target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation of /ɔː/ and reduced vowels.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes each group during their "Business Plan Explanation & Clarification Session." Students also use a simple peer assessment checklist to evaluate the clarity and mediation skills of the presenting group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplicity of explanations for business concepts (e.g., 'marketing,' 'automation').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectiveness in rephrasing ideas and resolving misunderstandings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clarify tasks using examples or visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target grammar: first conditional, present perfect, future continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of recommended vocabulary (e.g., 'strategy,' 'innovation,' 'customer-focused').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, particularly of /ɔː/ sounds (e.g., 'launch,' 'cost') and reduced vowels (e.g., 'successful').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of professional language and respect for suggestions (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List one specific business concept you explained today and how you made it simpler for your classmates."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Describe one strategy you used to clarify a task or resolve a misunderstanding in your group."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about simplifying complex business ideas for others? What is still challenging for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what real-life situations outside of school could you use these mediation skills (simplifying, clarifying, rephrasing)?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of the mediation tasks worked particularly well? Were students able to effectively simplify explanations and resolve misunderstandings?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students who struggled with clarifying or rephrasing? For example, providing more sentence starters or specific mediation phrases."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did students integrate the target grammar (first conditional, present perfect, future continuous) and vocabulary naturally into their mediation? Was the pronunciation of /ɔː/ and reduced vowels evident?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "My Genius Innovations" theme. The mediation skills practiced will be crucial for future collaborative projects and presentations across different themes and subjects, preparing students for real-world communication scenarios, particularly in academic or vocational contexts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-4 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>